# Stable perovskite solar cells with efficiency of 22.6% via quinoxaline-based polymeric hole transport material

1Beijing National Laboratory for Molecular Sciences, CAS Key Laboratory of Organic Solids, Institute of Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100190, China; 2School of Chemical Science, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China; 3College of Chemistry, Green Catalysis Center, Zhengzhou University, Zhengzhou 450001, China; 4Department of Materials Science and Engineering, Monash University, Clayton, VIC3168, Australia; 5State Key Laboratory of Advanced Technology for Materials Synthesis and Processing, Wuhan University of Technology, Wuhan 430070, China; 6Foshan Xianhu Laboratory of the Advanced Energy Science and Technology Guangdong Laboratory, Xianhu Hydrogen Valley, Foshan 528216, China; 7Laboratory of Advanced Optoelectronic Materials, College of Chemistry, Chemical Engineering and Materials Science, Soochow University, Suzhou 215123, China
Poor stability of spiro-OMeTAD hole transport materials (HTMs) with dopant is a major obstacle for the commercialization of perovskite solar cells (pero-SCs). Herein, we demonstrate a series of quinoxaline-based D-A copolymers PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 as the dopant-free polymer HTMs for high performance pero-SCs. The D-A copolymers are composed of fluorothienyl benzodithiophene (BDTT) as D-unit, difluoroquinoxaline (DFQ) with different side chains as A-unit, and thiophene as $\pi$ -bridge, where the side chains on the DFQ unit are bi-alkyl for PBQ5, bi-alkyl-fluorothienyl for PBQ6, and alkoxyl for PBQ10. All the three copolymers are adopted as the dopant-free HTM in the pero-SCs. The planar $n{-}i{-}p$ structured pero-SCs based on $(\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}})_{0.98}(\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}})_{0.02}$ with PBQ6 HTM demonstrated the high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $22.6\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.13\mathrm{V}$ and FF of $80.8\%$ , which is benefitted from the suitable energy level and high hole mobility of PBQ6. The PCE of $22.6\%$ is the highest efficiency reported in the $n{-}i{-}p$ structured pero-SCs based on dopant-free D-A copolymer HTM. In addition, the peroSCs show significantly enhanced ambient, thermal and light-soaking stability compared with the devices with traditional spiroOMeTAD HTM.
## perovskite solar cells, hole transport materials, D-A copolymer, difluoroquinoxaline, stability
Citation: Lu C, Zhu C, Meng L, Sun C, Lai W, Qin S, Zhang J, Huang W, Du J, Wang Y, Li Y. Stable perovskite solar cells with efficiency of $22.6\%$ vi quinoxaline-based polymeric hole transport material. Sc China Chem, 2021, 64: 2035–2044, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-021-1084-y
## 1 Introduction
The rapid growth of energy consumption and related environmental problems put forward a strong demand for the development of clean and sustainable energy source. With the continuous breakthrough of photovoltaic performance in recent years, organic/inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (pero-SCs) have become the most potential photovoltaic devices because of their low cost and solution-processing. Since its first development in 2009, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the pero-SCs has increased rapidly to
$25.5\%$ recently [1–12]. The metal halide perovskite commonly used in the pero-SCs has the general formula of $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ , in which A is the monovalent cation, such as organic methylammonium $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+})$ , formamidinium $(\mathrm{(NH_{2})_{2}C H^{+}})$ ; B and X usually represent the divalent lead ion ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ and halogen anions, respectively [13–15]. The rapid development of pero-SCs is attributed to the excellent semiconductor properties of the metal halide perovskite materials, such as suitable bandgap, high absorption coefficient, small exciton binding energy, excellent charge carrier mobilities and bipolar carrier transport property [1,16–18]. However, due to the intrinsic nature of the ionic perovskite material, the peroSCs decay fast when exposed to high temperature, humidity, oxygen and long-term light illumination, which significantly hinders its commercialization [19–25].
In the pero-SCs, the interfacial charge transport layer materials between the electrode and perovskite active layer are also crucial for the effective extraction of charge carriers from the perovskite layer to the electrode and for achieving high power conversion efficiency (PCE) and high stability of the pero-SCs. So far, the most widely used hole transporting material (HTM) in the state-of-art high-performance $n{-}i{-}p$ pero-SCs is $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis[N,N-di(4-methoxyphenyl)amino]- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene spiro-OMeTAD [2,26]. However, spiroOMeTAD, as HTM, requires the ionic $p$ -type dopants, such as lithium bis(trifuoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) [27,28] or its combination with tris(2-( $1H.$ -pyrazol-1-yl)-4- tert-butylpyridine) cobalt (III) tri[bis(trifuoromethane) sulfonimide] (FK209) [29], to produce enough free carrier density and higher conductivity. Consequently, obvious disadvantages exist in this type of HTM, such as poor stability, high cost, necessity of moderate oxidation and the complexity of its doping process, which bring obstacles to the large-scale commercialization of the pero-SCs. Another commonly adopted HTM is poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) [5,30,31]. Similarly, up to now, in the high-performance n-i-p typed pero-SCs, doping process is also required for PTAA HTM due to its intrinsically high resistivity [32]. Most importantly, the hygroscopic nature of the HTMs with ionic dopant makes them sensitive to chemical degradation and negatively impacts the stability of the entire devices. Therefore, it is imperative to develop the highly stable HTMs with high stability and simultaneously ensure the high photovoltaic performance of the pero-SCs.
Conjugated polymers, with their excellent intrinsic stability and easily tunable photoelectric properties, have been used as efficient and stable HTMs for pero-SCs [33–49]. In the conjugated polymers, planar structured D-A copolymers composed of an electron-donating (D) unit and an electronaccepting (A) unit possess the advantages of high hole mobility and tunable electronic energy levels by selecting different D-unit and different A-unit with different side chains.
Especially, the conjugated D-A copolymers could possess more planar backbone and better crystallinity of the neat film, with easily achieved high mobility and conductivity. Lee et al. [44] reported a D-A type copolymer HTM asyPBTBDT with benzo[1,2-b:4,5:b0]dithiophene (BDT) as Dunits and benzothiadiazole (BT) as A-units, which achieved PCE of $18.3\%$ . Kim et al. [48] reported copolymer PTEG with introducing tetraethylene glycol (TEG) chains on the BT unit and the pero-SC with PTEG HTM achieved PCE of $19.8\%$ . Cai et al. [49] reported a series of carbazole and BTbased D-A copolymers, which have different lengths of alkoxy side chains on the BT unit and achieved the champion PCE of $19.1\%$ . Most importantly, the absence of ionic dopant in these conjugated polymer HTMs avoids the additional ionic diffusion into perovskite layer, which enhances the overall device stability. The dopants are usually not covalently bonded with the HTMs, which can cause ion diffusion and adversely impact the long-term stability [50–52]. Dopant-free HTM also significantly simplifies the fabrication process in the potential future large-scale production [53]. However, the PCE of the pero-SCs based on the D-A copolymer HTMs is still lagging behind compared with the one with traditional spiro-OMeTAD HTM, due to the limited conductivity of the HTM and the less optimized ohmic contact between the D-A copolymers HTM and the perovskite active layer. Therefore, it is very important to design new D-A copolymers for the application as HTM for further improving PCE and stability of the pero-SCs.
Among the various A-units in the D-A copolymers, quinoxaline units possess the advantages of low cost, easily attaching different side chains with multiple attaching positions on the unit [54–57]. Suitable electron-withdrawing ability of the quinoxaline units is favorable to get the appropriate energy level alignment with the perovskite to minimize the loss of electrical potential and interfacial carrier recombination. The quinoxaline unit possesses planar and rigid conjugated structure with weak electron-deficient characteristic, which is an ideal moiety to construct wide bandgap D-A copolymer donors with good conductivity. The easily modifiable molecular structure of quinoxaline units allows finely tuning the physicochemical properties of the molecules. In 2018, Sun et al. [54] reported a low-cost quinoxaline-based D-A copolymer donor PTQ10 with alkoxysubstituted difluoroquinoxaline (DFQ) as an A-unit and thiophene as a D-unit. Then, Meng et al. [58] combined PTQ10 and the doped PTAA as the double hole transporting layers with graded energy levels, and effectively improved the hole extraction in the planar $n{-}i{-}p$ pero-SCs with the PCE of $21.2\%$ . Actually, bithienyl-banzodithiophene (BDTT) is the most popular D-unit in the D-A copolymers [59]. Therefore, Sun et al. [60] further synthesized a new D-A copolymer PBQ10 with bi-alkyl-fluorothienyl-substituted BDTT as a D-unit and alkoxy-substituted DFQ as an A-unit, and PBQ10 as a donor demonstrated a high PCE of $16.34\%$ in the corresponding organic solar cells. Recently, we synthesized two new PBQ-series D-A copolymers PBQ5 and PBQ6 with bi-alkyl and bi-alkyl-fluorothienyl side chains respectively on the DFQ A-unit [61], and we found that the electronic energy levels and hole mobilities of the polymers can be tuned effectively by the different side chains.
In this work, we applied the three PBQ copolymers (PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10, their molecular structures are shown in Figure 1a) as dopant-free HTMs in the planar $n{-}i{-}p$ pero-SCs in considering their appropriate hole mobilities and electronic energy levels, and studied the effect of the side chains of the DFQ unit on their photovoltaic performance in the pero-SCs. The three copolymers show gradually downshifted energy levels, and PBQ6 has the lowest HOMO (highest occupied molecular orbital) level and the best alignment with perovskite active layer (Figure 1c). Moreover, PBQ6 has the strongest intermolecular interaction, highest hole mobility and lowest interfacial carrier recombination in the pero-SCs. The pero-SCs with polymer PBQ6 as HTM without doping demonstrated a high PCE of $22.6\%$ , which is the highest PCE reported in the planar peroSCs based on the dopant-free D-A copolymer HTM. At the same time, the large-area $(1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ pero-SCs with bladecoated PBQ6 HTM fabricated in the ambient air achieved a PCE of $21.1\%$ and demonstrated good processibility. Most importantly, the pero-SCs devices with PBQ6 HTM showed significantly enhanced ambient, thermal and light-soaking stability compared with the traditional spiro-OMeTAD HTM, making PBQ6 a promising HTM for potential commercialization of the pero-SCs.
## 2 Results and discussion
Molecular structures of the polymer HTMs (PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10) are shown in Figure 1a. The synthetic procedures of PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 are following the previously published literature [60,61]. All of the three D-A copolymer HTMs PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 are composed of the BDTT with bi-alkyl-fluorothienyl side chains as D unit, DFQ with different side chains as A-unit and thiophene as the $\pi$ -bridge. Molecular weights of the three polymers are measured by high temperature gel permeation chromatography (GPC) as shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information online). The number-average molecular weights $(M_{\mathrm{n}})$ of the polymers PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 are 30.3, 28.7 and $19.2\mathrm{kDa}$ with polydispersity index (PDI) of 1.9, 1.4 and 2.0, respectively. All of the polymer HTMs show good solubility in common organic solvents, such as chloroform and chlorobenzene at room temperature, indicating good solutionprocessing capability. Figure 1b and Figure S2 show the molecular simulations on the backbone of the three polymers, and the dihedral angles between the D and A units were calculated to be $27.60^{\circ}$ , $14.19^{\circ}$ and $22.53^{\circ}$ in PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10, respectively. PBQ6 has the smallest dihedral angle in the three polymers, while PBQ5 contains the largest dihedral angle of $27.60^{\circ}$ due to the high repulsion force induced by the alkyl side chains.
For the application as HTMs in the pero-SCs, sufficiently deep energy level is the pre-requisite condition to form an optimal energy alignment with the valence band maximum (VBM) of the perovskite active layer. The VBM energy levels $(E_{\mathrm{VBM}})$ of PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 were determined by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS), and the values of $E_{\mathrm{VBM}}$ were measured to be $-5.31$ , $-5.43$ and $-5.25\mathrm{~eV},$ respectively (Figure S3). According to the previous report, the optical bandgaps of PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 were 1.88, 1.71 and $1.93\mathrm{eV},$ respectively [61]. The conduction band minimum (CBM) energy level $(E_{\mathrm{CBM}})$ could be calculated by $E_{\mathrm{CBM}}=E_{\mathrm{VBM}}-E_{\mathrm{g}}.$ In comparison, the $E_{\mathrm{VBM}}$ of PTAA is $-4.9\mathrm{eV}$ for reference. The perovskite layer adopted in this work contains an optimal composition of $(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})_{0.98}$ - $(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3})_{0.02}$ with the optical bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of $1.48\mathrm{eV},$ determined by the Tauc plot based on the UV/vis absorption spectra as shown in Figure S4. Figure 1c shows the energy level alignment between the perovskite active layer and the HTMs. The lower $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}(-5.43\ \mathrm{eV})$ of PBQ6 is mainly due to the strong electron-deficient effect of two alkylfluorothiophene side chains on the DFQ unit, which is beneficial for obtaining better energy alignment with the perovskite active layer, reducing the energy loss of charge carriers extracted from perovskite and achieving the higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ in the pero-SCs.
Generally, perovskite active layer has sufficiently high charge carrier mobility and long exciton diffusion length to efficiently transport the free carriers along the perovskite film, but the matching HOMO level with the perovskite active layer and the higher hole mobility remain a challenging issue when selecting HTMs. In addition, the charge transport capability of HTMs plays a critical role in determining the overall performance of the pero-SCs. Hereby, the hole mobilities $(\mu_{\mathrm{h}})$ of the three polymer HTMs PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 were measured using space-chargelimited current (SCLC) method with the hole-only devices (ITO/PEDOT:PSS/polymer $\mathrm{HTM/MoO_{3}/A g})$ ), and the measurement results are shown in Figure 1d. The $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ values of the copolymers PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 were measured to be
$2.2{\times}10^{-4}$ , $\phantom{+}4.6\times{10}^{-4}$ and $1.3{\times}10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are significantly higher than the undoped PTAA $({\sim}10^{-5}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{-}\mathrm{i}}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ [32]. The higher $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ of PBQ6 is in agreement with the DFT molecular simulations, and could be attributed to the more planar backbone of PBQ6 induced by the smaller angle between the BDTT unit and the bi-alkylfluorothienyl DFQ unit. The planar conformation should facilitate the formation of favorable intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and provide hole-transporting pathways in the PBM6 film (Table 1).
To further confirm the effect of side chains on the film morphology and orientation features of the polymer HTMs, the grazing incidence wide-angle $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray scattering (GIWAXS) was conducted. The results are included in Figures S5, S6 and Table S1 (Supporting Information online). The polymer HTMs PBQ5 and PBQ6 exhibit clear molecular crystalline order and preferential face-on molecular orientation with well-defined (010) $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking diffraction peaks in the out-of-plane (OOP) direction, which is supposed to contribute to efficient charge transport in the vertical direction of the substrate [61]. The crystal coherence length (CCL) of PBQ5 and PBQ6 in the OOP direction are calculated to be 15.09 and $19.20\textup{\AA}$ , respectively, indicating that PBQ6 exhibit the higher degree of molecular self-assembly and crystallinity, which is favorable to the charge transport and also consistent with the hole mobility results.
To further examine the potential of the PBQ-series polymers as the HTM in the pero-SCs, the $n{-}i{-}p$ planar-structured pero-SCs were fabricated with the device structure: ITO/ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite/polymer $\mathrm{HTM/MoO_{3}/A g}$ (Figure 2a), where the conventional polymer HTM PTAA (doped with tetrakis(pentafluorophenyl)borate (TPFB)) was used in the control devices. In this study, a uniform formamidiniumbased perovskite $((\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}})_{0.98}(\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}})_{0.02})$ active layer was deposited via the anti-solvent approach, which is more ideal for single-junction pero-SCs with narrower bandgap and higher PCE [10,62]. Fabrication details of the (FAP$\mathbf{bI}_{3})_{0.98}(\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3})_{0.02}$ pero-SCs are described in Supporting Information online. The cross-sectional image of the perovskite and HTM layers were acquired by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) as shown in Figure 2b, and the achieved perovskite layer reached the thickness over $700\mathrm{nm}$ , which could guarantee the full absorption of the solar spectrum in the range of 300 to $800\mathrm{nm}$ and maximize the short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ .
All of the polymer HTMs were processed in their pristine films without any dopants. The thickness of the HTM films was firstly optimized by changing the concentration of the polymer solutions in chlorobenzene, and the optimal thickness was determined to be ca. $60\mathrm{nm}$ . In Figure 2b, the SEM image shows that the HTM layer covers on top of the perovskite active layer as a uniform layer. To confirm the effective coverage, atomic force microscopy (AFM) was carried out to investigate the film formation morphology of the polymer HTM on top of the perovskite films (Figure S7). The root-mean-square (RMS) roughness was determined to be $24.0\ensuremath{\mathrm{nm}}$ for the perovskite layer. The full coverage of the underlying perovskite is critical to prevent the contact between the metal electrode and perovskite layer and to ensure the ideal ohmic contact. Additionally, the polymer capping layer could also protect the perovskite layer from the moisture in atmosphere and potentially enhance the environmental stability of the pero-SCs.
Figure 2c shows the current density-voltage $(J{-}V)$ curves of the optimized pero-SC devices with HTM of PTAA, PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 respectively, measured under the illumination of AM1.5G $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ at a scan rate of $0.05\mathrm{V~s}^{-1}$ . The detailed optimized photovoltaic parameters are shown in Table 2. The PBQ5-based devices achieved a PCE of $20.8\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.09\mathrm{V},J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $24.3\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and FF of $78.5\%$ , and the PBQ10-based devices achieved a PCE of $19.7\ \%$ with $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.08\mathrm{V},J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $23.8\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and FF of $76.6\%$ . Most importantly, the PBQ6-based devices demonstrated the champion PCE of $22.6\%$ $\cdot V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ : 1.13 V, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ : $24.7\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , FF: $80.8\%$ , which is one of the highest reported efficiencies in the planar pero-SCs based on dopantfree polymer HTMs. The PCE distribution of about 50 devices was included in Figure S8. For comparison, the PCE of the controlled device based on PTAA HTM was only $18.5\%$ 1 $(V_{\mathrm{oc}};~1.05~\mathrm{V},$ $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ : $23.8~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , FF: $74.2\%$ ). The performance of regular spiro-OMeTAD based pero-SCs was included in Table S2 and Figure S9. For the PBQ6-based devices, a stabilized PCE of $22.4\%$ at the maximum power point (MPP) tracking was achieved as shown in Figure 2c. The higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ (1.13 V) of the device based on polymer PBQ6 as HTM is benefitted from its better energy alignment and enhanced hole extraction. Moreover, the high intrinsic hole mobility of PBQ6 ensures the superior charge transport capability and leads to a greatly improved FF of $80.8\%$ . External quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the devices are shown in Figure 2e. All of the pero-SCs demonstrated high EQE values in the entire wavelength range from 420 to $800\mathrm{nm}$ . The integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the devices based on PBQ5, PBQ6, PBQ10 and PTAA as HTM are 23.84, 24.37, 23.36 and $23.40\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, which matches well with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values measured from $J_{-}V$ characteristics. The relation between photovoltaic performance and molecular weight of PBQ6 was further studied as shown in Table S3, and larger molecular weight of PBQ6 would exhibit better photovoltaic performance.
The performance of devices with various thickness of polymer HTM was screened as shown in Table S4. The optimized thickness of the HTM is $60\mathrm{nm}$ . For PBQ5 and PBQ6 HTMs, the devices showed relatively better thickness tolerance from $60{-}90~\mathrm{nm}$ due to the higher hole mobility. While the poorer performance of the devices with too thin HTM layer (such as $30\mathrm{nm},$ ) could be due to the partial contact between perovskite and the electrode with increased shunting paths because of the rough surface of the perovskite layer. In addition, due to the good film formation property and thickness tolerance, PBQ6 HTM has shown outstanding processibility with blade coating technique. The pero-SCs with larger area of $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ were fabricated in the ambient air with blade-coated PBQ6 HTM. The large-area device achieved a PCE of $21.1\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.09\mathrm{V},$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $24.6\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and FF of $78.9\%$ (see $J_{-}V$ curve shown in Figure 2d). The blade-coated PBQ6 HTM demonstrated excellent reproducibility and the ambient stability, which holds great potential in realizing the future commercialization of the pero-SCs.
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were conducted to investigate the effect of charge transport and recombination in pero-SCs based on polymer HTMs. EIS spectra was measured at the applied bias of $0.8{\mathrm{V}}.$ In the EIS analysis, the high-frequency component is the signature of the transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{tr}})$ , and the lowfrequency component is associated with the recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ . The plots of all the HTMs samples showed a single semicircle without transmission line behavior (Figure S10). In this study, since the ETL/perovskite interface is identical for all devices, the only variable affecting the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ is the perovskite/HTL interface. Notably, the devices with PBQ6 exhibited greater recombination resistance than others, which could effectively suppress the charge recombination and improve the device performance. The greater recombination resistance with PBQ6 can be explained by the fact that the PBQ6 has proper energy level alignment with perovskite active layer and enabled easy extraction of holes at the perovskite/HTL interface.
The built-in voltage $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ in the pero-SCs is closely related to trap density states and the overall quality of the interfaces. In order to determine the built-in voltage $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ in the pero-SC devices, the capacitance-voltage measurement was carried out using the Mott-Schottky analysis based on the following relationship:
$$
\frac{1}{C^{2}}=\frac{2}{q A^{2}\varepsilon N}(V_{\mathrm{bi}}-V)
$$
where $C$ is the capacitance, $q$ is the unit charge, $A$ is the device area, $\varepsilon$ is the dielectric constant and $N$ is the doping density [63]. From Figure 2f, the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ values for devices based on PBQ5, PBQ6, PBQ10 and PTAA were extracted as 1.076, 1.102, 1.046 and $1.015\mathrm{V}$ respectively. The higher $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of $1.102\mathrm{V}$ for the PBQ6-based device, suggests the less carrier recombination at the interface of HTM the highly efficient charge transport in the wide depletion region. The results are also consistent with the trend of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ in the pero-SC devices.
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) characterization was utilized to further investigate the interfacial hole transfer dynamics between perovskite and polymer HTMs. The incident laser with wavelength of $532\mathrm{nm}$ was applied to excite the perovskite films with and without HTMs. As shown in Figure 3a, the hole extraction ability of perovskite/HTM junction can be estimated by the quench of PL intensity of the perovskite film. Compared with the bare perovskite film, steady-state PL intensity of the perovskite films with PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 as hole extraction layers was largely reduced by more than half of the magnitude, and particularly for the perovskite/PBQ6 system, the PL was quenched by about $90\%$ . The large degree of quenching effect represents an efficient hole transfer process and the superior hole extraction capability of the polymer HTMs, which matched well with the characterized photovoltaic performance as mentioned above. In addition, the PL peak position slightly blue-shifted from 811 to $804\mathrm{nm}$ after applying PBQ6 to the perovskite surface. The slightly blue-shifted PL peak could indicate the less populated sub-bandgap trap states at the perovskite/polymer interface and the reduced Shockley Reed Hall (SRH) trap-assisted recombination. As reported by Cai et al. [49], the electron-rich side chain group, such as thiophene side chains of the polymer HTMs, could serve as the electron-donating Lewis bases to passivate the defect such as uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}$ atoms on the surface of the perovskite films. The slightly red-shifted PL peak in PTAA based device is due to the abundant trap states at the perovskite/ polymer interface and the undesired trap-assisted recombination.
Urbach energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{u}}\right)$ is an important and widely adopted parameter to indicate the energetic disorder in semiconductor materials and the defect densities in the tail states, which significantly influence the trap-assisted recombination and the performance of solar cells [64]. Hereby, Urbach tails of the perovskite active layers covered with PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 were detected by UV/vis spectra, as shown in Figure S11. Then the Urbach energies were deducted from the steepness of the absorption spectrum as 26.5, 24.6 and $27.4~\mathrm{meV},$ for the perovskite active layers covered by PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10, respectively. The lowest value for the perovskite active layer covered by PBQ6 confirms a cleaner interface between the perovskite active layer and the PBQ6 HTM with less sub-bandgap trap states, which is consistent with the photoluminescence results and high PCE of the PBQ6-based pero-SC.
Moreover, the charge transfer dynamics between the perovskite active layer and the HTM can be further confirmed by the time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decay curves. The decay lifetimes for bare perovskite and the perovskite/HTM (perovskite covered by HTM) are fitted with the biexponential model: $P L_{t}=A_{1}\mathrm{exp}(-t/\tau_{1})+$ $A_{2}\mathrm{exp}(-t/\tau_{2})+A_{0}$ , where $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ are the lifetimes for slow and fast decay and $\boldsymbol{A}_{1}$ and $\textstyle A_{2}$ are their relative amplitudes, respectively [65]. As shown in the time resolved PL spectra (Figure 3b), the shortened PL lifetime of the perovskite/ PBQ6 can be attributed to the enhanced charge extraction at the interface between the perovskite and the PBQ6 HTM due to the high hole mobility of PBQ6 and well-alignment interface. The decay lifetimes of the perovskite covered with different polymer HTMs are summarized in Table S5 and the observed trend is similar with the steady-state PL. Thus, better photovoltaic performance can be expected for the PBQ6-based pero-SCs.
The charge carrier lifetime and charge carrier extraction ability of the polymer HTMs were further analyzed by transient photocurrent (TPC). Figure 3c shows the photocurrent decay profile under the short circuit condition for the devices. From the TPC plots of different pero-SC devices based on the HTMs of PBQ5, PBQ6, PBQ10 and PTAA, the charge carrier lifetimes (τ) were extracted as 0.72, 0.62, 1.20 and $1.10~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , respectively. The decay time of the PBQ6 based devices was measured to be the shortest and almost half of the values for the PBQ10 or PTAA based devices, indicating that the charge can be more quickly swept-out from the perovskite/PBQ6 interface and PBQ6 could provide superior charge carrier extraction capability as HTM. The results are also consistent with the PL quenching analysis and the high FF in the photovoltaic performance.
To investigate how the carrier recombination process of devices is affected, the light intensity $(I)$ dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ was measured as shown in Figure 3d. The corresponding charge carrier recombination process could be reflected by the ideality factor of $n$ derived from the slope of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}{-}\mathrm{ln}(I)$ . The ideality factor $n$ in the devices based on PBQ6 HTM was extracted as 1.28, which was lower than the other comparison devices. It indicates that the Shockley-Read-Hall recombination is largely suppressed at the surface of light absorber and interface of PBQ6 HTM.
Besides superior photovoltaic performances, maintaining long-term stability has been one of the most challenging issues, since the perovskite crystals are vulnerable to various factors such as the sensitivity to moisture and oxygen. The polymer HTM-based devices should not only obtain high photovoltaic performance, but also preserve the stability of perovskite film substantially. Hereby, to examine the longterm stability of the pero-SCs with PBQ6 as HTM, the PCE of unencapsulated devices was monitored when stored in ambient air with a humidity of ${\sim}35\%$ , with the decay profile of photovoltaic performance as shown in Figure 4a. The pero-SCs with PBQ6 as HTM showed no obvious drop of performance after one month exposure in ambient air. The plausible reason for the improved ambiance stability of the PBQ6-based devices is the hydrophobic nature of the conjugated polymer HTM with alky side chains. The wetting angles of water on the surface of polymer HTMs were measured as shown in Figure S12. All of the quinoxalinebased polymers have larger wetting angles than PTAA. Particularly, PBQ6 has largest wetting angle among these samples and could effectively protect perovskite layer from ambient moisture. Moreover, to further examine the overall long-term stability in the possible real-life application, the conditions of continuous light-soaking and heat stress must be considered. The decay profile of photostability of peroSCs under the 1-sun illumination is shown in Figure 4b. After over 700 hours of continuous exposure under light, the devices showed superior stability and kept ${\sim}97\%$ of their initial PCEs. The thermal stability test was conducted for the as-prepared devices at $85~^{\circ}C$ in the nitrogen-filled glovebox as shown in Figure 4c. The devices with PBQ6 HTM showed excellent heat resistance and the target device performance retained ${\sim}93\%$ of original efficiency after continuous thermal stress at $85~^{\circ}C$ for over 700 hours, while devices with spiroOMeTAD HTM decayed rapidly within merely 10 hours (Figure S13). The property of thermal stability is mostly attributed to the intrinsic stability. Since there is inert atmosphere in the glovebox, the PCE loss for the spiroOMeTAD based pero-SCs is probably attributed to the accelerated diffusion of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ionic dopants in the spiro-OMeTAD HTM. Additionally, the overall stability of devices with PTAA was included in Figure S14 and the stability was inferior to the one with PBQ6. To conclude, the PBQ6 HTM not only produces high PCE of the pero-SCs, but also significantly enhances the long-term stability, making it a promising HTM for potential commercialization of the peroSCs.
## 3 Conclusions
In this study, to explore the potential of the quinoxalinebased D-A copolymers for the application as HTMs in peroSCs, the D-A copolymers of PBQ5, PBQ6 and PBQ10 with various side chains on DFQ units were used as HTMs in the planar $n{-}i{-}p$ structured pero-SCs based on $(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})_{0.98}.$ $(\mathrm{MAPbBr}_{3})_{0.02}$ . The side chains on the DFQ units are two alkyl for PBQ5, two alkyl-substituted fluorothiophene for PBQ6 and one alkoxy for PBQ10. All of the three copolymers possess higher hole mobilities and appropriate HOMO energy levels, and particularly PBQ6 shows the highest hole mobility and the lowest HOMO level and the best alignment with perovskite active layer. The pero-SCs based on (FAP$\mathbf{bI}_{3})_{0.98}(\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3})_{0.02}$ with polymer PBQ6 as HTM without doping demonstrated a high PCE of $22.6\%$ with high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.13\mathrm{V}$ and high FF of $80.8\%$ , which is the highest PCE reported in the pero-SCs based on dopant-free D-A copolymer HTM. At the same time, the large-area $(1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ pero-SCs with blade-coated PBQ6 HTM fabricated in the ambient air achieved a PCE of $21.1\%$ and demonstrated good processibility. In addition, the pero-SCs show significantly enhanced ambient, thermal and light stability. To conclude, the quinoxaline-based D-A copolymers, with their appropriate photoelectric properties and excellent intrinsic stability, have proven to be good HTMs and hold great potential in solving the stability issue in the field of pero-SCs, which could facilitate the future commercialization of the pero-SCs.
